What is Hash Dumping?

Hash dumping is the process of extracting password hashes from a system.

⚠️ Important concept:

Passwords are not stored in plain text — they’re stored as hashes

Attackers try to dump hashes so they can:

Crack them offline

Reuse them in pass‑the‑hash attacks

What is a Hash?

A hash is a one‑way encrypted value of a password.

Example:

password123 → 482c811da5d5b4bc6d497ffa98491e38


👉 You can’t reverse it directly, only guess & compare

Why Hash Dumping is Dangerous

Reveals user & admin credentials

Enables lateral movement

Leads to privilege escalation

Used in Active Directory attacks

Common Types of Password Hashes
🪟 Windows

LM (very weak – deprecated)

NTLM (still widely used)

🐧 Linux

MD5

SHA‑256

SHA‑512

Where Hashes Are Stored
Windows

SAM (Security Account Manager)

LSASS process memory

Linux

/etc/shadow

Hash Dumping Methods (Conceptual)
1️⃣ Local Hash Dumping

When attacker already has access to the system

Admin/root privileges required

Targets:

SAM database

Memory

2️⃣ Remote Hash Dumping

From another system over the network

Uses SMB / authentication mechanisms

Often used in domain environments

3️⃣ Memory-Based Dumping

Hashes extracted from RAM

Very dangerous because hashes exist temporarily in memory

What Happens After Dumping Hashes?
🔓 Hash Cracking

Using:

Wordlists

Rules

Brute force

Goal:

hash → password

🔁 Pass-the-Hash (PtH)

❗ No cracking required

Attacker uses:

NTLM hash = authentication


➡️ Logs in without knowing password

Defensive Perspective (Very Important for Exams)
How to Prevent Hash Dumping

🔐 System Level:

Disable LM hashes

Use strong passwords

Enable Credential Guard

Apply patches

🔐 Network Level:

Limit admin privileges

Monitor lateral movement

Use MFA

🔐 Monitoring:

Detect abnormal LSASS access

Log authentication attempts

Hash Dumping vs Password Cracking
Feature	Hash Dumping	Password Cracking
Purpose	Extract hashes	Recover passwords
Needs access	Yes	No (offline)
Speed	Fast	Slow
Tools (Awareness Only – Exam Relevant)

Credential dumping frameworks

Password cracking tools

Memory analysis tools

(You don’t need commands unless you’re in a lab like TryHackMe)

Key Exam / Interview Points ⭐

Hash ≠ password

Hash dumping needs privileged access

NTLM hashes can be reused

Pass‑the‑hash bypasses cracking
